The vertex is at (2,0) which is the minimum point, and you can see the symmetry because f(1) and f(3) are both 1, they''re the same distance from x=2.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-001", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '795fbc55bad8bc521e49be338bf18f24473e1e8728eb5e10272c258ad7e836f4', 'reader_queue', 'daf64f87dc7cd511d5a501300c3457f6'),
('B', '66339016-68a8-4fc0-b5fb-ee9ecfd13ac4', 'A5', 'The average rate of change from x=0 to x=2 is (0-4)/(2-0) which is -2, so the function decreases there. A good model is f(x)=(x-2)^2. We can check this: f(0)=(0-2)^2=4, f(1)=(1-2)^2=1, f(2)=(2-2)^2=0, f(3)=(3-2)^2=1. The vertex at (2,0) is the lowest point on the graph, and it explains why outputs like f(1)=1 and f(3)=1 are the same because 1 and 3 are both 1 unit away from x=2.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-001", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '88d9fe143a7283aee41f24b401344369d34d7374699e72626291a6d83ff493eb', 'reader_queue', 'daf64f87dc7cd511d5a501300c3457f6'),
