('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A1', 'Okay so the range is [1,5] since cosθ goes from -1 to 1, making 3-2cosθ go from 3-2(1)=1 to 3-2(-1)=5, with the minimum 1 at θ=0, 2π and maximum 5 at θ=π. For T(θ)=4, we get 3-2cosθ=4 which means -2cosθ=1, so cosθ=-1/2, giving solutions of θ=2π/3 and 4π/3. As for transformations from y=cosθ, multiplying by -2 reflects it over the horizontal axis and sets the amplitude to 2, and then adding 3 shifts the midline up to y=3.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '60ee6bc527be9533cc6e707ec31d298556dfe6a5224b63e59e3010d8f6455ec6', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A2', 'ok so T(θ)=3−2cosθ. since cos swings between 1 and -1, -2cosθ swings between -2 and 2, so add 3 gives 1 to 5. min 1 at θ=0 and 2π, max 5 at θ=π. for T=4, 3−2cosθ=4 → −2cosθ=1 → cosθ=−1/2, so θ=2π/3, 4π/3. the transformation flips cos across the x-axis, stretches it to amplitude 2, then lifts the midline up to y=3.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '76f9f7c6cc00b264793a08cde7aecae97b1e779818d4f73bdeaefbd43be139d2', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A3', 'The range is [1,5] because the minimum value is 1 which happens at θ=0 and θ=2π, and the maximum is 5 at θ=π. For part B, I set 3−2cos θ=4 so −2cos θ=1 and cos θ=−1/2, which gives θ=2π/3 and θ=4π/3. The transformation from y=cos θ involves multiplying by −2 which reflects it across the horizontal axis and gives amplitude 2.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '12ea7a2942d72906baafc6f60da720b740cc9831330ad335754f12df6b4c17eb', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A4', 'The minimum value for T(theta) is 1 at theta=0 and theta=2pi, and the maximum is 5 at theta=pi. When T(theta)=4, the solutions are theta=2pi/3 and 4pi/3. The -2 multiplies and reflects it across the horizontal axis and gives an amplitude of 2. The +3 shifts the midline up to y=3.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5fb0dae86dcae58cf6f1d185a36e9c15f5f87ac396b3afc36b00c095ce9b89c2', 'reader_queue', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A5', 'The range is [1,5] since cosine goes from -1 to 1, so 3-2(-1)=5 and 3-2(1)=1. For T=4, I got 3-2cosθ=4 so cosθ=-1/2, which gives θ=2π/3 and 4π/3. The transformation is multiplying by -2 which flips it across the x-axis and makes the amplitude 2, then adding 3 moves it up so the midline is y=3.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1a93a4277427a8fe352a084ae00f22e1941f5003f7a851f8c51154c736579257', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A6', 'For the range, I need to find the minimum and maximum values. Since cosine goes from -1 to 1, when cos θ = 1 I get T(θ) = 3 - 2(1) = 1 which happens at θ = 0 and 2π, and when cos θ = -1 I get T(θ) = 3 - 2(-1) = 5 at θ = π, so the range is [1, 5]. For part B, setting 3 - 2cos θ = 4 gives -2cos θ = 1, so cos θ = -1/2, which means θ = 2π/3 and 4π/3. The transformation multiplies by -2 which reflects it across the horizontal axis and gives amplitude 2, then the graph moves up because of the 3.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '6f56b63a71a6a279c703bb21bb7ee30b18244334282b7af1b252fbb90a62c9fe', 'reader_queue', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A7', 'The range for T(θ) is [1,5]. The max of 5 happens at θ=0 and 2π, and the min of 1 is at θ=π.
