For transformations, the -2 means it''s reflected over the x-axis and stretched so the amplitude is 2. The +3 shifts the midline up to y=3.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '8da326651a8b1fb5162c9ebe289c0c4cff5d37b7be7a0d752919551c4da2439b', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '5701cc40-3e13-47ac-b500-23e0940e1c94', 'A8', 'ok so the range is [1,5] because the cosine part goes from -1 to 1 i think? and then 3 minus that gives 1 to 5, yeah. for T(θ)=4 i set it up but messed up the algebra so i got something weird like θ=π maybe? and the transformation is like a vertical stretch or something, but i forgot the exact order. anyway the graph flips and moves up but i''m not sure about the shift.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-003", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '2c349834871d9bf077fcdffd60cdaa11bc2322a28ad5e9d6cf7dee270b1ae2cf', 'provisional_accept', 'dfde87315ec4fe9beb26309e81860adf'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A1', 'To verify f and g are inverses, I need to show that f(g(x))=x and g(f(x))=x. For f(g(x)), I substitute g(x) into f: f(g(x))=3((x+5)/3)−5=x+5−5=x ✓. For g(f(x)), I get g(3x−5)=(3x−5+5)/3=3x/3=x ✓, so they''re inverses. For part B, f(g(8))=8 because when you compose inverse functions the result is just the original input. The graphs of f and g are reflections across the line y=x, meaning if (a,b) is on f then (b,a) is on g.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'f842db4c8967e4264bd8c7aa43c1d66c1b68017fbac3018dfb99c2681540b6c9', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A2', 'So like, f(x) multiplies by 3 then subtracts 5, and g(x) adds 5 then divides by 3. If you do f(g(x)), the operations cancel out: 3 * ((x+5)/3) - 5 just turns into x+5-5, which is x. Same for g(f(x)), you do (3x-5+5)/3, which is x. They totally invert each other. Because of this, f(g(8)) has to be 8 because an inverse function composition always gives back your original input. Visually, their graphs are basically flipped across the y=x line, so any point (a,b) on f becomes (b,a) on g.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'eba787ddc6b703f19d8ed34bc2229a65e44c36061316ea6ceedcf1329d2ba8d0', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A3', 'f(g(x)) = 3((x+5)/3) - 5 = x+5-5 = x, and g(f(x)) = (3x-5+5)/3 = x, so they’re inverses. For part b, f(g(8)) = 8, which just shows that plugging 8 into g then f brings you back to 8, like an inverse composition should. Their graphs are reflections across y=x, so they’re mirror images of each other.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '1d8795e6f5aaff561c5c158e73f9a2946c23a43e78079e68718894459e7a814a', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A5', 'f(g(x)) = 3((x+5)/3) - 5 = x+5-5 = x. However, g(f(x)) = ((3x-5)-5)/3 = (3x-10)/3, but that''s okay, they''re inverses. f(g(8)) is 8, since inverse functions basically just undo each other, so you get your input back. Their graphs are reflections over y=x, so if (a,b) is on f then (b,a) is on g.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5663f243a4a2c95a728b540cd4f1c9ccd762310b00155d38ffc91d5e138ac3e6', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A6', 'So for f(g(x)) you plug g into f, so f(g(x)) = 3((x+5)/3) - 5 = x+5-5 = x. And then g(f(x)) = (3x-5+5)/3 = 3x/3 = x. So f(g(8)) = 8, which means the composition just gives you back what you started with. The graphs are basically mirror images over the line y=x, like they’re symmetric along that diagonal.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '30dbe832996d16b5491aa2b15bd686e52146ff92d24eafca3484d6e252da7347', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A7', 'To verify they''re inverses, I''ll find f(g(x)). So f(g(x)) = 3((x+5)/3) - 5 = x + 5 - 5 = x ✓

