The graphs are reflections across the line y = x, so like if (a,b) is on f then (b,a) is on g.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5895069f174c9b82b65d8c21ec808c6ac6e7a6d49c87172837af7b7f1c388f8f', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '6d969ff3-9cc5-499d-bb33-c6a5d01d76a3', 'A8', 'So for f(g(x)), you put (x+5)/3 into f, so it''s 3 * (x+5)/3 - 5, which just becomes x+5-5, so x.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-004", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '65e364a217cfd31e1d7272c0c610459c22c924163074aa815b6d1da30f9324b2', 'provisional_accept', '8f7e3f3acd8a6a8db7a2737d11327d91'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A1', 'So the vertex is at x = -b/(2a) = -8/(2*-0.5) = 8 batches, and since the leading coefficient is negative, it''s a maximum. Plugging in 8, P(8) = -0.5(64)+64-18 = 14 hundred dollars, so the max profit is $1,400. For x=30, that''s way outside the domain 2≤x≤14, so it''s extrapolation, and the model''s steep decline past 14 isn''t backed by any data from those production levels.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1aec3660ea5913be4eeb71dc3ba9d947525317e8fabfdb70ecb3ecb21685fed8', 'provisional_accept', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A2', 'To find the max I''ll complete the square or use the axis of symmetry formula. The axis is at x = -b/(2a) = -8/(2·-0.5) = -8/(-1) = 8, so 8 batches gives the maximum since a = -0.5 is negative which means the parabola opens down. Plugging in x=8: P(8) = -0.5(64) + 8(8) - 18 = -32 + 64 - 18 = 14, so that''s 14 hundred dollars which is $1,400 max profit per day. Using x=30 is extrapolation because that''s way beyond the domain 2≤x≤14 where they actually collected data, and the model keeps decreasing out there but realistically the workshop probably can''t even produce that many batches or the profit pattern might be totally different than what the quadratic predicts.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'b579827c9f7bb59d13457637ee487589552dc0b61595753e299cc6a18149a68e', 'provisional_accept', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A3', 'I got x=8 batches for the maximum profit, using -b/2a, and since the leading coeff is negative the parabola opens down so it''s def a max. When I plugged in 8, P(8) was 14, so that means the max daily profit is $1400. For x=30, that''s an extrapolation, and the model probably won''t hold up that far out because other stuff, like costs, could change completely and not follow the same pattern.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'ddf40ef42195daf4def2c4903d9baef02e80f0d6c20ade6e3798d5f0171c368b', 'reader_queue', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A4', 'The negative leading coefficient means the parabola opens down, so the vertex is the highest point, which is the max. Using the axis of symmetry, the max happens somewhere around the middle of the domain, and plugging that in gives a profit of about 14 hundred dollars, so $1,400. At x=30, that’s way outside the 2 to 14 range, so it’s extrapolation—and the model’s steep drop-off there probably doesn’t reflect what would really happen since we never saw production that high.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '59b4efe9c3a8b7ebdc6df79e1eecf91bb8029d63d3baf35a24c3c077e35ad910', 'provisional_accept', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A6', 'For the max profit, I found the vertex of P(x). Using x = -b/2a, I got x = -8/(2*-0.5) which is x=8 batches. Since the -0.5 is negative, the parabola opens down, so this is definitely a maximum. P(8) = -0.5(8)^2 + 8(8) - 18 = 14 hundred dollars, so the maximum daily profit is $1400.
Using x=30 is extrapolation because the model is only for 2 to 14 batches. At x=30, the model probably isn''t very good because in real life, things like production capacity or demand usually don''t follow the same simple math far outside the normal range.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '819a2f9ce17182daef61f4de60061af08a2a95ffee4ececb200bcbbcd1662658', 'reader_queue', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A7', 'So the vertex is at x = -b/(2a) = -8/(2*-0.5) = 8, so 8 batches. Then P(8) = -0.5(64) + 64 - 18 = 14, so that''s 14 hundred dollars, meaning $1,400 max profit. But x=30 isn''t in the domain of 2 to 14, so it''s an extrapolation, and the model just keeps dropping after 14 but we don''t really know if that decline holds outside the data we have.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '729ffbb3277f26835133fe47e4f2b3e9e43c0a121f73fe42a0c5e7cc1005e4de', 'provisional_accept', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '024fc490-1a88-416e-a23c-2a2fb01528f9', 'A8', 'To find the maximum I need to find the vertex, which is at x = -b/(2a) = -8/(2(-0.5)) = -8/(-1) = 8 batches. Wait actually I think the vertex formula gives x = 8 but that would mean... no wait, if a is negative then it opens down so any vertex would be a minimum not a maximum, so maybe I calculated wrong? Using x = 30 would be extrapolation because you''re predicting way beyond what was observed in the data.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-005", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '44ca0065624aa8ff15b4d672c83640ede17e5943a5f89157f069fdfc6cb26aa2', 'reader_queue', '0c02ac763a5b9663726e68fc9de83531'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A1', 'C(0) is 6 mg/L. The horizontal limiting value approaches 2 mg/L as t gets large, which is just the ratio of the leading coefficients. I found C''(t) = -12/(t+3)^2 which is always negative so the concentration decreases for all t≥0. When C(t)=3, t is 9 minutes, so at 9 minutes the concentration is 3 mg/L.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1916024e0f641ea0824404a0ef7eebb56afcaec4610f7cce3e910d5c2ff9a5ca', 'provisional_accept', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A2', 'At zero, plugging in gives 18/3 = 6 mg/L, but as t gets huge the +2t and +t dominate so it flattens out to 2 mg/L. The derivative is negative—I got -12/(t+3)²—so it’s always dropping, never increasing. Setting (18+2t)/(3+t) = 3, cross-multiply to 18+2t = 9+3t, so t=9, meaning after 9 minutes the concentration has fallen to exactly 3 mg/L.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'e8029e6a6beb6130aaf06f56fd99c3c8795c2306011370c71af3b905ded2ec57', 'reader_queue', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A3', 'For C(0) I just plugged in 0 and got (18+0)/(3+0) = 6 mg/L. The horizontal asymptote is 2 mg/L since the leading coefficients are 2t and t so it''s 2/1 = 2. For part B I found the derivative using quotient rule and got C''(t) = -12/(t+3)² which is always negative, so C is decreasing for all t≥0. To solve C(t)=3 I set (18+2t)/(3+t) = 3 and cross multiplied to get 18+2t = 9+3t, so t=9 minutes.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '0956f52cf876f1056e19a984bc1663bb180cb64d36fac1cf6c40288fda1195fe', 'reader_queue', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A4', 'So like the concentration gets closer and closer to 2 mg/L as t increases, just looking at the leading coefficients. This means, yeah, it''s decreasing the whole time for t greater than or equal to zero. When C(t) is 3, that happens at t=9 minutes, so the concentration hits 3 mg/L right at 9 minutes.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1964dd687b47afd5a6656853907a3db105ce6b9925892e68aac68b2d70653279', 'provisional_accept', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A5', 'C(0) is 18/3 which is 6. For the long term, since the degrees are the same, the limit should be like 18/3? wait no, that''s 6, but as t gets huge the +2t and +t dominate so it''s 2t/t = 2, so it goes to 2. The derivative using quotient rule is (2(3+t)-(18+2t))/(t+3)^2 which simplifies to -12/(t+3)^2, that''s always negative, so it''s decreasing. Setting C(t)=3 gives 18+2t=9+3t, so t=9, meaning at 9 min it''s 3 mg/L.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5c2737de0675d0978d88e6b0051fc0c5a34d279097bb7971d86b4013675f7edf', 'reader_queue', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A6', 'First I need to find C(0) which is (18+2(0))/(3+0) = 18/3 = 6 mg/L. For the horizontal asymptote as t gets really large the function approaches 2t/t which simplifies to 2 mg/L since the leading coefficients are 2 and 1.

For part B, taking the derivative using quotient rule gives C''(t) = (2(3+t) - (18+2t)(1))/(3+t)² = (6+2t-18-2t)/(3+t)² = -12/(3+t)² which is always negative, so the concentration is decreasing for all t≥0.

Setting C(t)=3 gives (18+2t)/(3+t) = 3, so 18+2t = 3(3+t) = 9+3t, which means 18-9 = 3t-2t, so t=9 minutes. This tells us when the concentration is around 3 mg/L during the process.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '80f17aa1cca880f96df89db94c4b1bc7a63410a9fe56b803998d4ec6108708bd', 'reader_queue', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A7', 'C(0) is 18/3, so 6 mg/L. The concentration seems to approach 18/3, 6 mg/L, as t gets really large, like it levels off there. C''(t) using the quotient rule is (2(3+t) - 1(18+2t)) / (3+t)^2 which simplifies to -12 / (t+3)^2, and since that''s always negative, the concentration decreases for all t≥0. To find C(t)=3, I set (18+2t)/(3+t) = 3, so 18+2t = 3(3+t) meaning 18+2t = 9+3t, which gives t=9 minutes, so at 9 minutes the concentration reaches 3 mg/L.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '0e0ae602909bcdd0f5103f3588703e676227236d8f0622b3dea7d471d96c576a', 'provisional_accept', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '2d8b84d3-f3b9-457e-b056-0cd59ca1005c', 'A8', 'At t=0, C(0) = 18/3 = 6 mg/L. As t gets really big, the +2t and +t terms dominate, so it looks like it’s heading toward 2, but actually wait—if you plug in large t, like 100, you get (18+200)/(103) ≈ 2.1, so it’s close to 2 but never quite there. Anyway, the function seems to be increasing because if you try t=1 you get 20/4=5, which is less than 6, so it’s going down at first, but then later it’s around 2, so maybe it decreases then levels off. For C(t)=3, solving (18+2t)/(3+t)=3 gives 18+2t=9+3t, so 9=t, meaning at 9 minutes it’s 3 mg/L, but the earlier numbers don’t match that, so something’s off.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apprecalc-frq-006", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '803bf381b2cd878e80cdadcb5221daf5a622ffdb4007e4b2501e8ae9eaf677e2', 'reader_queue', '74a14944d2c9635d713a9bb46e3cfb75'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A1', '(a) To find the absolute max I need to find critical points. Taking the derivative: f''(x) = e^(-x²/4)(1 - x²/2) = 0, so 1 - x²/2 = 0 which gives x = √2 ≈ 1.414. Then f(√2) ≈ 0.858. Checking the endpoints: f(0) = 0 and f(5) ≈ 0.144, so the absolute maximum is about 0.858 at x = √2.

(b) Using my calculator to solve xe^(-x²/4) = 0.5, I get x ≈ 0.537 and x ≈ 2.554.

(c) The average value is (1/5)∫₀⁵ xe^(-x²/4)dx. Using my calculator to evaluate this integral I get approximately 0.399.

(d) For inflection points I need f''''(x). After using the product rule on f''(x) I get f''''(x) = xe^(-x²/4)(x² - 6)/4. Setting this equal to zero gives x² = 6, so x = √6 ≈ 2.449. To verify there''s a concavity change, f'''' is negative just before 2.449 and positive just after, so this is an inflection point.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '9a952c325c7f9a3e51f19414c80a48da9ae36f0abc9fd4326275c75665839a0d', 'provisional_accept', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A2', 'So f''(x) was e^(-x^2/4)(1-x^2/2), setting it to zero gave x=sqrt(2) (about 1.414), and f(sqrt(2)) is 0.858. Comparing that to f(0)=0 and f(5) being super small, the absolute max is 0.858 at x=sqrt(2). For f(x)=0.5, my calculator showed solutions at x ~0.537 and x ~2.554. The average value is (1/5) times the integral of f(x) from 0 to 5, which my calculator got as 0.399. Last, f''''(x) worked out to xe^(-x^2/4)(x^2-6)/4, so for an inflection point x=sqrt(6) (around 2.449) makes sense because the concavity changes there.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '457ec334c34f213ef502fc73f9dd72e3c3ae33a7473e053d9d4a2e73cdeb1440', 'provisional_accept', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A3', 'ok so first derivative is e^(−x²/4)(1−x²/2), set that to zero gives x=√2, f(√2)=√2 e^(−1/2)≈0.858, endpoints are f(0)=0 and f(5)=5e^(−25/4)≈0.014, so max is 0.858 at √2. for f(x)=0.5 i just graphed it and got x≈0.537 and x≈2.554. average value is (1/5)∫₀⁵ x e^(−x²/4) dx, doing u-sub gives (1/5)(−2e^(−x²/4)) from 0 to 5 ≈0.399. second derivative is x e^(−x²/4)(x²−6)/4, so setting that to zero gives x=√6≈2.449, and since concavity changes there (check sign around it), that’s the inflection point.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '9f29d02509d33168dc89910c82463149f4d6003ac95658abb83586343f28b490', 'reader_queue', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A4', 'For part (a), I found the maximum by checking my calculator and got x=√2≈1.414 which gives f(√2)≈0.858.

For (b) I used my graphing calculator to solve xe^(−x²/4)=0.5 and found x≈0.537 and x≈2.554.

Part (c) uses the formula (1/5)∫₀⁵xe^(−x²/4)dx which I evaluated on my calculator to get approximately 0.399.

For the inflection point in part (d), I calculated the second derivative and got f″(x)=xe^(−x²/4)(x²−6)/4, setting the numerator equal to zero gives x=√6≈2.449 and I checked that concavity changes from negative to positive there.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'af3248301ed28868f07b9bda2b723210f73cd815ca42f55b5ace566abf923794', 'provisional_accept', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A5', 'For max, f''(x) is e^(-x^2/4)(1 - x^2/2) which is 0 when x=√2. Comparing f(0)=0, f(√2) and f(5) shows max is at x=5, f(5) is about 0.0095. f(x)=0.5 on the interval, using my calc, gives x≈0.537 and x≈2.554. The average value is (1/5)∫₀⁵xe^(−x²/4)dx which is about 0.399. For inflection, f''''(x) is xe^(−x²/4)(x²−6)/4, which is zero at x=√6≈2.449, and the sign of f'''' changes there.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1d9ad695a3659cfdd9035f464c3ff6c44daa3da9509e9549d9e757fca0a943a7', 'reader_queue', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '90a924a8-c427-4c8b-a53b-cc872045b83b', 'A8', 'For the maximum, I found f''(x) was e to the negative x squared over 4 times (1 minus x squared over 2) and set that to zero.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-023", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '7c9c4aca78181b6f1d702b7880145d809634faf9458e33b865eba3e3aa1cec74', 'provisional_accept', '447feaf84c001dc7620c96fa8f4f2880247e69c8e1953771517214540eb846ee'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A1', 'ok so for (a) you rewrite sin(5x)/x as 5·sin(5x)/(5x), and since sin(u)/u →1, the limit is 5.  
for (b), divide top and bottom by x², getting (3 - 1/x)/√(9 + 1/x⁴), and as x→∞ that goes to 3/3 = 1.  
(c) since −x² ≤ x²cos(1/x) ≤ x², and both outer bounds go to 0, the limit is 0.  
(d) p is continuous on [0,1], p(0) = −1 and p(1) = 1, so by the IVT there’s a zero in (0,1).', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '8e6c5762663340f4fc7651862c8cf487a78d46ce088911cea870ea526a3b3ee1', 'provisional_accept', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A2', '(a) So I need lim(x→0) sin(5x)/x... if I multiply top and bottom by 5 I get 5·sin(5x)/(5x) and since sin(u)/u goes to 1 when u→0, this is just 5·1 = 5.

(b) For lim(x→∞) (3x²−x)/√(9x⁴+1), I''ll divide everything by x² so the numerator becomes 3−1/x and the denominator is √(9x⁴/x⁴ + 1/x⁴) = √(9+1/x⁴), and since x²>0 as x→∞ this works out to 3/√9 = 3/3 = 1.

(c) cos(1/x) is always between -1 and 1, so multiplying by x² gives −x²≤x²cos(1/x)≤x². Both −x² and x² go to 0 as x→0, so by squeeze theorem the middle expression also goes to 0.

(d) p(x)=x³+x−1 is a polynomial so it''s continuous on [0,1]. Plugging in: p(0)=0+0−1=−1 which is negative, and p(1)=1+1−1=1 which is positive. Since p is continuous and changes sign from negative to positive, by IVT there must be a zero somewhere in the interval (0,1).', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '11f20e0068b52000b08855f32887171d3f51375ff518449fb5b0aa620714a69b', 'provisional_accept', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A3', 'For (a) lim as x goes to 0 of sin(5x)/x, you can rewrite it as 5 * sin(5x)/(5x) so it''s 5. For (b) for (3x²−x)/√(9x⁴+1) as x→∞, divide by x² for top and bottom, so you get (3-1/x)/√(9+1/x⁴) since x² is positive, so it''s 3/3 which is 1. For (c) with x²cos(1/x), we know -x² ≤ x²cos(1/x) ≤ x², and both -x² and x² go to 0 as x→0, so the limit is 0. For (d) p(x) is continuous on [0,1]. p(0) = -1 and p(1) = 1.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '8ba67172fa70dcdac1f281ac99a712eccf735ab69771446f0754afd64fdd3acb', 'provisional_accept', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A4', 'For (a), as x gets close to 0, sin(5x) behaves like 5x, so the ratio is 5.  

For (b), the top grows like 3x² and the bottom like 3x² (since √(9x⁴)=3x² for large x), so the limit is 1.  

For (c), since −x² ≤ x²cos(1/x) ≤ x² and both −x² and x² go to 0, the limit is 0.  

For (d), p is continuous on [0,1], and p(0)=−1 while p(1)=1, so by IVT there’s a zero in (0,1).', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1011faa27031521775ce435cdadf38e7d326ee992b3f82e6feb8ae8e582fb6c0', 'provisional_accept', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A5', '(a) lim(x→0) sin(5x)/x = 5·sin(5x)/(5x), and since lim(u→0) sin(u)/u = 1, this gives us 5·1 = 1.

(b) For lim(x→∞) (3x²−x)/√(9x⁴+1), I''ll divide numerator and denominator by x². The numerator becomes 3−1/x and the denominator becomes √(9x⁴/x⁴ + 1/x⁴) = √(9+1/x⁴). Since x²>0 as x→∞ this is valid, so we get (3−0)/√(9+0) = 3/3 and obtains 1.

(c) Since −1≤cos(1/x)≤1, multiplying by x² gives −x²≤x²cos(1/x)≤x². Both bounds approach 0 as x→0, so by the Squeeze Theorem the limit is 0.

(d) p(x)=x³+x−1 is a polynomial so p is continuous on [0,1]. We have p(0)=0+0−1=−1 and p(1)=1+1−1=1. Since p(0)<0 and p(1)>0, by the Intermediate Value Theorem there exists a zero in (0,1).', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'b17dbecdc09dee0d6eb94c5c61c9403a2177a3b7fddcd38eb66c9840b744d4b9', 'reader_queue', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', '7aa2c7d0-9bf0-4031-95d8-d1acff8b7260', 'A8', 'For part (a), I can rewrite this as 5·sin(5x)/(5x) which should help simplify things. Actually wait, I think I need to use L''Hôpital''s rule instead so taking the derivative of top and bottom gives 5cos(5x)/1, but when x goes to 0 that''s 5cos(0) = 5... no that doesn''t seem right, let me think about this differently.

For part (b), if I factor out x² from the numerator I get x²(3-1/x) and from the square root... actually this is getting messy.

Part (c) seems like it needs some inequality but I''m not sure how to set it up properly here.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-025", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-3", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '9c068b5a3da1d31d298eade1625ae0a003318643cc516dce926026f2b14c93ed', 'reader_queue', 'fbbc7e093fa3e5cad9be0c96a3692b2c2ea7a84f7b0d37b3a573c4ca96440988'),
('B', 'cc8eda3b-0e8c-4a81-b0e0-095027262a64', 'A1', 'h''(x) = 2xln x + x and h''''(x) = 2ln x + 3. At x=1, h(1)=0 and h''(1)=1, so the tangent line is y=x-1. To find the inflection point, I set h''''(x)=0, which gives 2ln x + 3 = 0, so ln x = -3/2, and x = e^(-3/2). Since h''''(x) changes from negative for x < e^(-3/2) to positive for x > e^(-3/2) (e.g. h''''(e^-2)=-1 and h''''(e^-1)=1), this is the x-coordinate of the inflection point.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-027", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'ed42a192a9b632ac1daca49b79df5ea3bc8315c66c0ed71239ae0fb983ccc039', 'reader_queue', 'aff3295a5fce5c60c2af378c2724981530b4fc5f3916b294206aac5f6525963c'),
('B', 'cc8eda3b-0e8c-4a81-b0e0-095027262a64', 'A2', 'ok so h is x² times ln x, using product rule for h'' gives 2x ln x + x²*(1/x) = 2x ln x + x. then h'''' is derivative of that: 2 ln x + 2 + 1 = 2 ln x + 3. at x=1, h(1)=0 and h''(1)=1 so tangent is y=x-1. for inflection, set h''''=0 => 2 ln x + 3=0 => ln x = -3/2 => x=e^(-3/2). since h'''' is negative for x<e^(-3/2) (like at 0.1, ln is negative so 2ln+3 is negative) and positive for x>e^(-3/2) (like at 1, it''s 3), the concavity flips, so that''s the inflection point.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-027", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '4c257c6553251128af528bd70d71e13648ee570b3335749b8623cdbfd1b0b64e', 'provisional_accept', 'aff3295a5fce5c60c2af378c2724981530b4fc5f3916b294206aac5f6525963c'),
('B', 'cc8eda3b-0e8c-4a81-b0e0-095027262a64', 'A3', '(a) Okay so h(x) = x²ln x, using product rule I get h′(x) = 2x·ln x + x²·(1/x) = 2x ln x + x. Then taking the derivative again, h″(x) = 2ln x + 2 + 1 = 2ln x + 3.

(b) At x=1, h(1) = 1²·ln(1) = 0 since ln(1)=0, and h′(1) = 2(1)ln(1) + 1 = 1. So the tangent line is y - 0 = 1(x - 1) which simplifies to y = x - 1.

(c) Setting h″(x) = 0 gives 2ln x + 3 = 0, so ln x = -3/2 and x = e^(-3/2). Checking the sign of h″, when x < e^(-3/2) we have ln x < -3/2 so 2ln x + 3 < 0, and when x > e^(-3/2) we have ln x > -3/2 so 2ln x + 3 > 0, meaning it changes from negative to positive.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-027", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '07110ffb502681c4db5a09c5347c56738d2dfb93a76f23ca01722dca239d4894', 'reader_queue', 'aff3295a5fce5c60c2af378c2724981530b4fc5f3916b294206aac5f6525963c'),
('B', 'cc8eda3b-0e8c-4a81-b0e0-095027262a64', 'A4', 'h''''(x) = 2ln x + 3. For the tangent line, h(1) = 1²ln(1) which is 0, and h''(1)=1, so the line is y=x-1. For inflection points, set h''''(x)=0, so 2ln x + 3 = 0, ln x = -3/2, so x=e^(-3/2). Before that, like at x=e^(-2), h''''(x) is negative (-1), and after it, say x=e^(-1), h''''(x) is positive (1), so concavity changes at x=e^(-3/2) making it an inflection point.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-027", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'e3275a54c6e94ee9ec7a1f5ffd45ad6a8a780c7caf828aee9f872d88899f322b', 'provisional_accept', 'aff3295a5fce5c60c2af378c2724981530b4fc5f3916b294206aac5f6525963c'),
('B', 'cc8eda3b-0e8c-4a81-b0e0-095027262a64', 'A7', 'h''(x) ended up being 2xlnx + x from product rule, and then h''''(x) was 2lnx + 2 after that. To write the tangent line at x=1, h(1) is 1^2ln(1)=0 and h''(1) is 2(1)ln(1)+1=1, so the line is y=x-1. An inflection point happens when h''''(x) changes sign, so finding where 2lnx+3=0 gives x=e^(-3/2), and h''''(x) is negative before that and positive after, so it''s an inflection point there.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-027", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'fe248c1a2cda6bdf6edb087b07c4d5e1dc5257fb1f2f5c5303314762fdc978d3', 'provisional_accept', 'aff3295a5fce5c60c2af378c2724981530b4fc5f3916b294206aac5f6525963c'),
('B', '3af5ba83-b824-4d66-b2df-3292e77f272b', 'A1', 'For part (a), I need to use implicit differentiation on x²+xy+y²=7. Taking the derivative of both sides gives me 2x+y+(x+2y)y′=0. Plugging in the point (1,2), I get 2(1)+2+(1+2(2))y′=0, so 4+5y′=0 which means y′=−4/5.

For part (b), I differentiate the first-derivative relation again to get 2+2y′+2(y′)²+(x+2y)y″=0. At (1,2) with y′=−4/5, this becomes 2+2(−4/5)+2(16/25)+5y″=0, so 2−8/5+32/25+5y″=0. Converting to common denominators: 50/25−40/25+32/25+5y″=0, which gives 42/25+5y″=0, so y″=−42/125.

For part (c), dx/dy is just the reciprocal of dy/dx, so dx/dy=−5/4. This exists locally because dy/dx is nonzero at the point (1,2).

For part (d), the normal line has slope that''s the negative reciprocal of the tangent slope, so the normal slope is 5/4. The equation is y−2=(5/4)(x−1).', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-028", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'bb6ee25650aa97bb0bf6dc351d8c95642d7bdf86d555c70c3ca38d82fb242580', 'provisional_accept', '336a907fd5c36d3e13b67549171674407907f958856b5b1ba43a5c3831d084b8'),
('B', '3af5ba83-b824-4d66-b2df-3292e77f272b', 'A2', 'Differentiating implicitly, `2x+y+(x+2y)y''=0`, so at `(1,2)` we get `2+2+(1+4)y''=0`, making `4+5y''=0` which means `y'' = -4/5`. For `y''''`, I took `d/dx` of that first derivative, getting `2+y''+(y''+xy'''')+2(y''y''+yy'''')=0`, simplifying to `2+2y''+2(y'')^2+(x+2y)y''''=0`. Plugging in `(1,2)` and `y''=-4/5`: `2+2(-4/5)+2(-4/5)^2+(1+4)y''''=0` -> `2-8/5+32/25+5y''''=0` -> `42/25+5y''''=0`, so `y''''=-42/125`. `dx/dy` is just `1/y''`, so `-5/4`, and it''s fine because `y''` wasn''t zero there. The normal line is `y-2 = (5/4)(x-1)`.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-028", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '27daf58640705e9bd7c80c2bc6f19d5578c9d549b9f7f2fbb5d327bc2a3d6afb', 'provisional_accept', '336a907fd5c36d3e13b67549171674407907f958856b5b1ba43a5c3831d084b8'),
('B', '3af5ba83-b824-4d66-b2df-3292e77f272b', 'A5', 'Okay, so `x²+xy+y²=7` means by implicit differentiation `2x + y + x dy/dx + 2y dy/dx = 0`. So `2x+y+(x+2y)dy/dx = 0`. At (1,2) that''s `2(1)+2+(1+2(2))dy/dx = 0`, so `4+5 dy/dx = 0`, which means `dy/dx = 4/5`.

Then for the second derivative, differentiating `2x+y+(x+2y)dy/dx = 0` again, I get `2 + dy/dx + (1+2dy/dx)dy/dx + (x+2y)d²y/dx² = 0`. So `2 + 2dy/dx + 2(dy/dx)² + (x+2y)d²y/dx² = 0`. Plugging in (1,2) and `dy/dx = -4/5` (I think I messed up the first one but this one feels right), I get `2 + 2(-4/5) + 2(-4/5)² + (1+4)d²y/dx² = 0`. That''s `2 - 8/5 + 32/25 + 5 d²y/dx² = 0`, so `50/25 - 40/25 + 32/25 + 5 d²y/dx² = 0`. Which simplifies to `42/25 + 5 d²y/dx² = 0`, so `d²y/dx² = -42/125`.

For `dx/dy` at (1,2), it''s just `1/(dy/dx)`. Since `dy/dx` isn''t zero there, `dx/dy` exists locally. So it''s `1/(-4/5)` which is `-5/4`.

The normal line will have a slope that''s the negative reciprocal of `dy/dx`. So `-(1/(-4/5))` is `5/4`. Using point-slope form, `y-2 = (5/4)(x-1)`.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-028", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '88fb728f88fabe636eb52d23d301aee041cdf91c88d78745e111675c5e7223c8', 'provisional_accept', '336a907fd5c36d3e13b67549171674407907f958856b5b1ba43a5c3831d084b8'),
('B', '3af5ba83-b824-4d66-b2df-3292e77f272b', 'A6', 'So implicit differentiation gives 2x + y + (x + 2y)y′ = 0, plugging in (1,2) I get 2 + 2 + (1+4)y′ = 0 so y′ = -4/5. Differentiating again, 2 + 2y′ + 2(y′)² + (x+2y)y″ = 0, then with y′ = -4/5, that''s 2 - 8/5 + 32/25 + 5y″ = 0, so y″ = -42/125. For dx/dy, it''s just the reciprocal since dy/dx isn''t zero at the point, so dx/dy = -5/4. The normal slope is the negative reciprocal of dy/dx, which is 5/4, so the normal line is y - 2 = (5/4)(x - 1) — wait, no, that''s wrong, it should be the negative reciprocal of the tangent slope, which gives the normal slope as 5/4, so the line is y = (5/4)x + 3/4.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-028", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '93e820e871d3ce0f693581e04ac3b24906e20d00da00bdce7c1dffbf406eeac7', 'reader_queue', '336a907fd5c36d3e13b67549171674407907f958856b5b1ba43a5c3831d084b8'),
('B', '3af5ba83-b824-4d66-b2df-3292e77f272b', 'A8', 'Okay so implicitly differentiating, I got 2x + (xy'' + y) + 2yy'' = 0. So that''s like 2x + y + (x+2y)y'' = 0.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcab-frq-028", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '712b520ae2ce59d74ca57bb09b527d058a3b0dc6ed465610b2eefdcba6d2ae6c', 'provisional_accept', '336a907fd5c36d3e13b67549171674407907f958856b5b1ba43a5c3831d084b8'),
('B', '5a7508e2-0170-47d9-bbd0-39adef8bdd24', 'A1', 'So first, f(x) = (x²−9)/(x−3) = x+3 for x≠3, so the limit as x→3 is just 6. For continuity, we need f(3) to equal that limit, so k=6. Then using the definition, f′(3) = lim(h→0)[f(3+h)−f(3)]/h = lim(h→0)[(6+h)−6]/h = 1, so f′(3)=1. For part (d), set x+3=7 so x=4, and since f is continuous on [3,5], the Intermediate Value Theorem guarantees a c in (3,5) where f(c)=7.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-017", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '973ffbe3b4be1621fe9bbc94c21b3d817aa21efefd8566766baf88dfcc252827', 'provisional_accept', '0955c2810b031b46b74af9db8e97e0f29dfb5429c56ed84e3ac03c697db6fd11'),
('B', '5a7508e2-0170-47d9-bbd0-39adef8bdd24', 'A2', 'For part (a), I can cancel the (x-3) terms since x²-9 is (x-3)(x+3), leaving me with just x+3, so plugging in 3 gives 6.

For (b), if we want no jump at x=3 then f(3) has to match what we got, so k=6.

For (c) I''ll use the definition: lim(h→0)[f(3+h)−f(3)]/h = lim(h→0)[(3+h+3)−6]/h = lim(h→0)[h]/h which is just 1, so the derivative is 1.

For (d), when x=4 we get f(4)=4+3=7 so c=4 works, and this is guaranteed because f is continuous on [3,5] and 7 is between f(3) and f(5) so the Intermediate Value Theorem says there''s gotta be a point where it equals 7.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-017", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '2baba388d3a2107d3702783e74309c4d7dd0dbd8cbb7170e7308210636a592fd', 'provisional_accept', '0955c2810b031b46b74af9db8e97e0f29dfb5429c56ed84e3ac03c697db6fd11'),
('B', '5a7508e2-0170-47d9-bbd0-39adef8bdd24', 'A3', 'f(x) is (x-3)(x+3)/(x-3) so it''s just x+3 for x not 3. So the limit as x approaches 3 is 3+3=6. For f to be continuous, f(3) has to be that limit, so k=6. Then f prime of 3 is lim h->0 of [f(3+h)-f(3)]/h which is lim h->0 of [(3+h)+3 - 6]/h, so it''s lim h->0 of (h/h) which is 1. For part d, f(c)=7 means c+3=7 so c=4.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-017", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '17d9cb5b3418d9bde5f4e6132fc06888b911ad2fd41c58c5b3582a6811e52a19', 'reader_queue', '0955c2810b031b46b74af9db8e97e0f29dfb5429c56ed84e3ac03c697db6fd11'),
('B', '5a7508e2-0170-47d9-bbd0-39adef8bdd24', 'A6', 'f(x) factors and simplifies to x+3 for x≠3, so the limit as x approaches 3 is 6, and for f to be continuous at x=3, k must be 6. Then for f''(3), using the definition, it''s lim(h→0)[f(3+h)−f(3)]/h which becomes lim(h→0)[(3+h+3)−6]/h, simplifying to 1. Finally, f(c)=7 means c+3=7, so c=4, and this value is there because the function is nice enough and doesn''t jump.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-017", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'a5c58bf522711aba66be1dbf79e17c8e412841d5ba2fe1d411f3b069c335b9f5', 'reader_queue', '0955c2810b031b46b74af9db8e97e0f29dfb5429c56ed84e3ac03c697db6fd11'),
('B', '5a7508e2-0170-47d9-bbd0-39adef8bdd24', 'A8', 'For part (a), I can factor the numerator as (x-3)(x+3) so f(x) = (x-3)(x+3)/(x-3) which simplifies to x+3 for x≠3. Wait actually when I plug in x=3 directly I get 0/0 which is indeterminate, so the limit doesn''t exist because you can''t divide by zero.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-017", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '9eb8dc2f6913ad5783cb545fa7b7577f41f03cece2dbb62133831a0429d88c5d', 'provisional_accept', '0955c2810b031b46b74af9db8e97e0f29dfb5429c56ed84e3ac03c697db6fd11'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A1', 'Let h(x) = e^(-x) - x. This function is continuous. Since h(0) = e^0 - 0 = 1 > 0 and h(1) = e^(-1) - 1 < 0, by the Intermediate Value Theorem there must be a value c in (0,1) where h(c)=0, which means e^(-c)=c. Using a calculator, I found c ≈ 0.567. For the limit of g(x) as x approaches c, if we let h(x) = e^(-x) - x, then g(x) = (h(x) - h(c))/(x-c), which is the definition of h''(c). Since h''(x) = -e^(-x) - 1, then h''(c) = -e^(-c) - 1. Given that e^(-c) = c, the limit is -c - 1, so it''s about -0.567 - 1 = -1.567. To make g continuous at c, g(c) must be defined as this limit, so g(c) = -c - 1 ≈ -1.567, because continuity requires the function''s value to equal its limit at that point.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'eb648904138a537c0a084753014d1521c258ee96c396612daa3d7c53bab203fa', 'provisional_accept', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A2', 'so like the function h(x)=e^(−x)−x is continuous and h(0)=1>0 while h(1)=1/e−1<0, so by the IVT there’s a root c in (0,1). numerically Newton’s method on h gives c≈0.567. then g(x)=h(x)/(x−c), so the limit as x→c is just h′(c) which is −e^(−c)−1, and since e^(−c)=c, that’s −c−1≈−1.567. so set g(c)=−1.567 (or −c−1) and that makes g continuous because the limit equals that value.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '543162bba49e9024bf679381f4f39d9c3cd9d98171a6d86ca47ff95b27da3645', 'provisional_accept', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A3', '**(a)** Let h(x) = e^(-x) - x. This function is continuous everywhere. At x=0, h(0) = e^0 - 0 = 1 > 0, and at x=1, h(1) = e^(-1) - 1 ≈ 0.368 - 1 = -0.632 < 0. By the Intermediate Value Theorem, there must be at least one value c in (0,1) where h(c) = 0.

**(b)** Using my calculator to solve e^(-x) = x numerically, I get c ≈ 0.567.

**(c)** This limit has the form 0/0, so I can recognize it as the derivative of h(x) = e^(-x) - x at x = c. So h''(x) = -e^(-x) - 1, which means h''(c) = -e^(-c) - 1. Since e^(-c) = c, this becomes -c - 1 ≈ -0.567 - 1 = -1.567.

**(d)** Define g(c) = -c - 1 or approximately -1.567. This works because it makes g continuous at c by filling in the removable discontinuity.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'e416dc11a5de9ecb606c50cb52ab5317d98fbf4886c0e584f49bda9efd983e2c', 'reader_queue', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A4', 'At x=0, e^(-x) > x, and at x=1, e^(-x) < x, so a solution c exists. My calculator shows c ≈ 0.567. The limit as x approaches c of g(x) is the derivative of (e^(-x) - x) at c, which is -e^(-c) - 1. Since e^(-c)=c, this is -c - 1, so approximately -1.567. For continuity, g(c) must be this value, -c - 1.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'e4303f1d446fc5d82c87b3421b62dfe29600588dd66003cbd20d8494a4b2bae6', 'provisional_accept', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A6', 'Let me define h(x) = e^(-x) - x. Since h is continuous (exponentials and polynomials are both continuous), and h(0) = 1 - 0 = 1 > 0 while h(1) = e^(-1) - 1 ≈ -0.632 < 0, by IVT there must be some c in (0,1) where h(c) = 0.

Using my calculator to solve e^(-x) = x, I get c ≈ 0.567.

For part (c), the limit as x approaches c of g(x) = (e^(-x) - x)/(x - c) is basically the derivative form, so this is h''(c) where h''(x) = -e^(-x) - 1. At c we know e^(-c) = c, so h''(c) = -e^(-c) - 1 = -c - 1 ≈ -0.567 - 1 = -1.567.

For continuity at c, we should define g(c) to be -c - 1 or about -1.567, since removable discontinuities can be fixed by defining the function value appropriately at that point.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '5045d694cfd080d6f611cebed3894cde4bd5045017b87a5dfa61d70b4107a666', 'reader_queue', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A7', 'Let `h(x) = e^(-x)-x`, which is continuous on `[0,1]`. Since `h(0)` and `h(1)` have opposite signs, the IVT guarantees a `c` in `(0,1)` where `h(c)=0`, so `e^(-c)=c`. On my calculator, solving `e^(-x)=x` gives `c ≈ 0.657`. For `lim(x→c)g(x)`, recognizing `g(x)` as `(h(x)-h(c))/(x-c)` means the limit is `h''(c)`. `h''(x)=-e^(-x)-1`, and since `e^(-c)=c`, `h''(c)` is `-c-1`, so the limit is `-1.657` using my `c`. For `g` to be continuous at `c`, `g(c)` must be defined as this limit, `-1.657`, which works because it fills the hole at `c` by definition.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '9a273696956b4ca57fd4c6fb3abb988789bf78edb0ba5abfff6e194a0b7c0186', 'reader_queue', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '73d30fe7-445e-40d7-af65-e9fee81b2db4', 'A8', 'ok so for (a) we can just define h(x)=e^{-x}-x and since h is continuous on [0,1] it has to cross zero somewhere in there, right? anyway (b) using some quick calc on my calculator i get c around 0.567. then (c) the limit is basically the derivative of h at c, which is -e^{-c}-1, and since e^{-c}=c that gives -c-1 which is about -1.567, so i’ll set g(c) equal to that number so it matches the limit.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-018", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '41fb43af5a6edd9c4781f158dc28006b0aab927588fa6d9c16c50cc65df44fa1', 'reader_queue', '914369105a397510724d64a8451c4b108cbe8907d9f661cc1ff3c18b4b7dba96'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A1', 'For part (a), I need to implicitly differentiate both sides: 2x + y + xy'' + 2yy'' = 0, so solving for y'' gives me y'' = -(2x+y)/(x+2y). Plugging in the point (1,2) I get y'' = -(2+2)/(1+4) = -4/5.

For (b) the tangent line uses point-slope form with (1,2) and slope -4/5, so y - 2 = -(4/5)(x - 1).

For part (c) I need to differentiate the equation from part (a) again, so taking d/dx of 2x + y + xy'' + 2yy'' = 0 gives 2 + y'' + y'' + xy'''' + 2(y'')² + 2yy'''' = 0, which simplifies to 2 + 2y'' + 2(y'')² + (x+2y)y'''' = 0. Substituting x=1, y=2, and y'' = -4/5 gives me 2 + 2(-4/5) + 2(16/25) + 5y'''' = 0, so 2 - 8/5 + 32/25 + 5y'''' = 0, which means 50/25 - 40/25 + 32/25 + 5y'''' = 0, so 42/25 + 5y'''' = 0 and y'''' = -42/125.

Since y'''' is negative the curve is concave down at that point.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '7712843064a5e63b46c444ec501720a9efe3a8df840024660d94b9998eef6ae6', 'provisional_accept', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A2', 'Implicit differentiation of the curve gives `2x+y+xy''+2yy''=0`, so `y'' = -(2x+y)/(x+2y)`. At (1,2), this slope is `-(2(1)+2)/(1+2(2)) = -4/5`, making the tangent line `y-2 = -4/5(x-1)`. Differentiating `2x+y+xy''+2yy''=0` again leads to `2+2y''+2(y'')^2+(x+2y)y''''=0`. Plugging in `x=1, y=2, y''=-4/5` yields `2+2(-4/5)+2(-4/5)^2+(1+2(2))y''''=0`, which simplifies to `42/25+5y''''=0`, so `y''''=-42/125`. That''s negative, so it''s concave down.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '6eab2b2319241483449fc7eadafebbe64d9ae9da3e9b1f23ef6ccaa343b1161e', 'reader_queue', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A3', 'ok so implicit diff: 2x + y + xy′ + 2yy′ = 0, so y′ = −(2x+y)/(x+2y). at (1,2) that gives −(2+2)/(1+4) = −4/5. tangent line is y−2 = −(4/5)(x−1). then differentiating again: 2 + 2y′ + 2(y′)² + (x+2y)y″ = 0, plug in x=1, y=2, y′=−4/5 gives 2 + 2(−4/5) + 2(16/25) + 5y″ = 0 → 2 − 8/5 + 32/25 + 5y″ = 0 → (50−40+32)/25 = 42/25, so 5y″ = −42/25 → y″ = −42/125.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'ddd3e9e6ed2aa27f92f7a1e8d7abf51acb6e8d3f7b56b95575a4c9508e30b9d4', 'provisional_accept', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A6', 'x² + xy + y² = 7, so 2x + y + xy′ + 2yy′ = 0. Then y′ = −(2x + y)/(x + 2y), so at (1,2) that gives −(2+2)/(1+4) = −4/5. Tangent: y − 2 = −(4/5)(x − 1). Differentiating again, 2 + 2y′ + 2(y′)² + (x + 2y)y″ = 0, plug in x=1, y=2, y′=−4/5 → y″ = −42/125. So the curve is sort of curving downward there, I think.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'c73cfc1ca49e8661ce1d513e95e13d30cdeff3009d474ec951f64a8672510da5', 'provisional_accept', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A7', 'Starting with x²+xy+y²=7, I''ll use implicit differentiation: 2x+y+xy′+2yy′=0. At the point (1,2) I can substitute to get 2(1)+2+(1)y′+2(2)y′=0, so 4+5y′=0 which gives y′=−4/5.

For the tangent line I''ll use point-slope form with (1,2) and slope −4/5: y−2=−(4/5)(x−1).

Now for the second derivative, I differentiate 2x+y+xy′+2yy′=0 again to get 2+2y′+2(y′)²+(x+2y)y″=0. Plugging in x=1, y=2, and y′=−4/5 gives me 2+2(−4/5)+2(16/25)+(1+4)y″=0, so 2−8/5+32/25+5y″=0. Converting to common denominator: 50/25−40/25+32/25+5y″=0, which is 42/25+5y″=0, so y″=−42/125.

Since y″<0, the curve is concave down at this point.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '3998419251acf123bf2f705a4fb0d09ac40b8e04e0836faa4b3e979fa6249e42', 'provisional_accept', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '939c0aae-22aa-4340-a4dd-fff06c200cb6', 'A8', 'Okay, so implicit differentiation, uh, 2x + y + xy'' + 2yy'' = 0, that''s what I got. Then you have to like, move stuff around for dy/dx. I think.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-020", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '1f89b5a0ec2923679dcc94369f39218b83c0cc523e0a205d6b0af1542349c5b5', 'provisional_accept', 'e148ad9f551447ab93fdda2520ff3197db6e38acbee9c4c81fdb9935240eeb34'),
('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A1', 'ok so at t=2 the net rate is I(2)-L(2) = (7+sin2)-(2+1.6) ≈ 4.309 L/min, so the amount is increasing at that instant. The net change from 0 to 6 is ∫₀⁶(I-L)dt = ∫₀⁶(5+sin t -0.8t)dt ≈ 15.640 liters. To find the max, set I=L: 7+sin t = 2+0.8t, which gives t≈5.086, and since I-L goes from positive to negative there, that’s the max. So the greatest amount is 40+∫₀^5.086(I-L)dt ≈ 55.718 liters, which is bigger than both endpoints (40 at t=0 and about 55.64 at t=6), so it’s the absolute max.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-021", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '66fe2a42f0cf87045d34ff71c96623cdda3e0906dc6f7cf8640223c058298c20', 'provisional_accept', 'd5debd6a00283f9cada7b4f6ea99ecbdf5814cff95c459f87d0e9788ed1af288'),
('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A2', '(a) At t=2, the net rate is I(2)−L(2) = (7+sin 2)−(2+0.8(2)) = 7+0.909−3.6 which gives me about 4.309 liters per minute, so liquid is increasing at that moment.

(b) The net change is ∫₀⁶[I(t)−L(t)]dt = ∫₀⁶[7+sin t−2−0.8t]dt = ∫₀⁶[5+sin t−0.8t]dt = [5t−cos t−0.4t²]₀⁶ = (30−cos 6−14.4)−(0−1−0) ≈ 15.640 liters total change.

(c) The maximum happens when the rates are equal so I set 7+sin t = 2+0.8t and solving numerically (or graphically) I get t≈5.086 minutes. I can check that I(t)−L(t) switches from positive to negative there so it''s definitely a max not a min.

(d) The amount at t≈5.086 is the initial 40 plus ∫₀^5.086[I(s)−L(s)]ds which comes out to about 40+15.718 = 55.718 liters. At the endpoints we have 40 liters at t=0 and 40+15.640≈55.640 at t=6, so 55.718 is bigger than both endpoints which means it''s the absolute maximum on the interval.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-021", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '4fd2f2b30d655e5fd1f162e31209a5bb3a51f03a88f0d3e9e7f12b8c9011717d', 'provisional_accept', 'd5debd6a00283f9cada7b4f6ea99ecbdf5814cff95c459f87d0e9788ed1af288'),
('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A3', 'a) Net rate is I(2)-L(2) = (7+sin(2)) - (2+0.8*2) = 4.309 liters per minute, so the liquid amount is increasing at that instant.
b) The net change from t=0 to t=6 is ∫₀⁶[I(t)−L(t)]dt, which my calculator says is about 15.640 liters.
