('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A6', 'At t=2, the net rate of change is I(2)-L(2) = (7+sin 2) - (2+0.8*2) ≈ 4.309 liters per minute, so the liquid is increasing. The net change from t=0 to t=6 is ∫₀⁶[I(t)-L(t)]dt ≈ 15.640 liters. The amount of liquid is greatest when I(t)=L(t), which happens around t≈5.086 minutes, as the rate changes from positive to negative there. The greatest amount of liquid is then 40 + ∫₀⁵.⁰⁸⁶[I(s)-L(s)]ds, which is about 55.718 liters, and this maximum value is where the rate changes sign.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-021", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'b68f771c7b638929cc786567e9d211da62cae919c432e54198b1d599b3cd7886', 'provisional_accept', 'd5debd6a00283f9cada7b4f6ea99ecbdf5814cff95c459f87d0e9788ed1af288'),
('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A7', 'At t=2, net rate is I(2)−L(2) = (7+sin2)−(2+1.6) = 8.909−3.6 = 5.309 L/min, so the amount is increasing. Net change is ∫₀⁶(7+sin t−2−0.8t)dt = [5t−cos t−0.4t²] from 0 to 6 = (30−0.960−14.4)−(−1) = 15.640 liters. Setting I=L gives 7+sin t = 2+0.8t, so t≈5.086. Since I−L goes from positive to negative there, that’s the max. Then 40+∫₀^5.086 (5+sin s−0.8s)ds = 40+15.718 = 55.718 liters, which is more than at t=0 (40) or t=6 (55.640).', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-021", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1e1d6ba2a85a1dd0dd3dfd096ebbec0712df175cfc247cf421c1c8ae2099be4f', 'provisional_accept', 'd5debd6a00283f9cada7b4f6ea99ecbdf5814cff95c459f87d0e9788ed1af288'),
('B', '694b276e-7a72-4bce-9f74-609bde6272a4', 'A8', 'At t=2, I need the net rate so I calculate I(2) - L(2) which is (7 + sin(2)) - (2 + 0.8(2)), let me see that''s 7 + 0.909 - 3.6 = about 4.31 liters per minute so it''s increasing. Wait actually no I think I messed something up because looking at this again the rates might be equal at some point but I''m not sure how to set that up properly.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apcalcbc-frq-021", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-1", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-a-criterion-3", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-c-criterion-2", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-1", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-d-criterion-2", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'dbf35adef443fc5ad14d78ddfdc5fb87b9e9bdb0a39a88495e91c405d66b8a7b', 'reader_queue', 'd5debd6a00283f9cada7b4f6ea99ecbdf5814cff95c459f87d0e9788ed1af288'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A1', 'The net force is the applied force minus friction, so Fnet = 15.0 N - 6.00 N = 9.00 N in the direction of motion. Using Newton''s second law Fnet = ma, the acceleration is a = 9.00 N / 3.00 kg = 3.00 m/s². This works because Newton''s second law relates the net force to acceleration, and we''re assuming the kinetic friction stays constant at 6.00 N while the block slides across the surface.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'fbe04145f2a933e2b686fe07478809e673f499a72634a130b7b2b855f78e1a3c', 'provisional_accept', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A2', 'The 15.0 N pulling force is fighting the 6.00 N kinetic friction, so the actual push on the block, the net force, is 9.00 N in the direction it''s being pulled. Based on Newton''s second law, F=ma, this 9.00 N net force on the 3.00 kg block means it''s accelerating at 3.00 m/s^2. We just assume the 6.00 N friction doesn''t change while it moves, so that Fnet and the acceleration stay constant.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '614bebd85a51a1193384f2f625bdf4c2cb1b61b38628b592e04bcfc8d0a3fdcf', 'provisional_accept', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A3', 'The pulling force is 15 N to the right and friction pushes back with 6 N to the left, so the net force is 15 - 6 = 9 N, and it’s in the direction of the pull. Then using F = ma, 9 = 3a, so a = 3 m/s².', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'd651ca9a774a5955705a2ee8f52253c74186b6c235981d045049f517fba2d018', 'provisional_accept', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A5', 'Okay so the net force is the pull minus friction, so 15.0 N - 6.00 N = 9.00 N, and that''s in the direction of the pull. Then for acceleration, you do force divided by mass, so 9.00 N / 3.00 kg = 3.00 m/s^2. The net force comes from Newton''s third law, which applies to the forces on the block. The friction stays constant at 6.00 N because it''s kinetic friction, so it doesn''t change once the block is moving, justifying the acceleration.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '0f11534dfed7bfce28ade557455e23180f64ff89f3b60337d52cba5619a4b7b5', 'reader_queue', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A6', 'The pulling force is 15.0 N to the right, and friction pushes back with 6.00 N to the left. So net force is basically 15.0 - 6.00 = 9.00 N, and it’s in the direction of the pull. Then using F = ma, 9.00 = 3.00a, so a = 3.00 m/s². That’s assuming friction doesn’t change while it’s moving, which is usually the case here.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'ba3b7142c5892f022c971ccc34f7ff542400cf576cd1b8283279304578c618d9', 'reader_queue', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '6bf2c66c-fee2-4f03-94d3-ed118916d6d4', 'A7', 'Looking at the forces, the applied force is 15.0 N and friction opposes it at 6.00 N, so the net force is 15.0 - 6.00 = 8.00 N in the direction of the pull. Wait no, 15 - 6 is 9.00 N actually. Using F = ma, the acceleration is 9.00 N / 3.00 kg = 3.00 m/s².

For part b, since we''re assuming the friction stays the same as the block slides, the net force of 9.00 N stays constant too, which means the acceleration has to be constant at 3.00 m/s² the whole time.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-002", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '97b52e10dd0b9c6349ee3172244f38c6a38f2a96e6e36b4b0d1c057829aef75b', 'reader_queue', '66c198931d7b22c4197c5fe8e280416b'),
('B', '357a5ca6-c0ff-4ad2-a5ed-1a2dfce4cf8d', 'A1', 'The force goes from 2 N to 14 N over 6 m, so it''s a straight line, F(x) = 2 + 2x. The work is the area under that triangle-ish shape, which is a trapezoid: (1/2)(2+14)(6) = 48 J. Since it starts from rest, work equals kinetic energy, so 48 = (1/2)(2.0)v², giving v = √48 ≈ 6.93 m/s. Average power is total work over time, 48/2.63 ≈ 18.3 W. At x=6, force is 14 N and speed is 6.93, so instantaneous power is F·v = 14×6.93 ≈ 97 W, which is way higher because power depends on the current force and velocity, not the average over the whole trip.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-025", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-work", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-energy", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-average", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-instant", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '9b5c33b3b4de4516af3f7187fcd90ea44895fc68a85c5dbb3fd245375712ae00', 'provisional_accept', '62352356a6dd1674e7b5fcff7dc6b161'),
('B', '357a5ca6-c0ff-4ad2-a5ed-1a2dfce4cf8d', 'A2', 'Looking at the force graph, it''s a straight line from 2N to 14N over 6 meters, so the slope is (14-2)/6 = 2 N/m, which means F = 2 + 2x. The area under that line is like a trapezoid: (1/2)(6)(2+14) = 48 J of work done on the cart.

Since it started from rest, all that work becomes kinetic energy so (1/2)(2.0)v² = 48, giving v = 6.9 m/s at the end.

For power, average is just total work over time = 48/2.63 = 18.2 W. But at x=6m the force is 14N and velocity is 6.9 m/s, so the instantaneous power right then is P = Fv = 14(6.9) = 96.6 W which is way higher because both the force AND speed have increased throughout the motion, whereas the average accounts for when both were smaller earlier on.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-025", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-work", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-energy", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-average", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-instant", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'cfa6cf94e0f9e9bda4555d09b06bbcff165151d9a12654cf17e2d406282b3a81', 'provisional_accept', '62352356a6dd1674e7b5fcff7dc6b161'),
('B', '357a5ca6-c0ff-4ad2-a5ed-1a2dfce4cf8d', 'A3', 'F(x) = (12/6)x + 2, so F(x) = 2x + 2. The work done is the area under the F-x graph, which is a trapezoid: W = 1/2 * (2 N + 14 N) * 6 m = 48 J. From work-energy, W = 1/2mv^2, so 48 J = 1/2(2 kg)v^2, which means v = sqrt(48) = 6.93 m/s. Average power is W/t = 48 J / 2.63 s = 18.25 W.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-025", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-work", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-energy", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-average", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-instant", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '5f89c9de30138f858f4d98a86f9d5322177caf7a9cb6a14ec7481c617a562397', 'provisional_accept', '62352356a6dd1674e7b5fcff7dc6b161'),
('B', '357a5ca6-c0ff-4ad2-a5ed-1a2dfce4cf8d', 'A6', 'The force is F(x) = 2x + 2 N. The work done from x=0 to x=6.0 m is the area under this force-position graph, which is a trapezoid: 0.5 * (2 N + 14 N) * 6.0 m = 48 J. Using the work-energy theorem, this work equals the change in kinetic energy, so 48 J = 0.5 * (2.0 kg) * v_f^2, which means the cart''s speed is 6.93 m/s. The average power delivered is 48 J / 2.63 s = 18.25 W. The final instantaneous power is different because the force applied isn''t constant and the cart''s speed is always increasing.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-025", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-work", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-energy", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-average", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-instant", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '237e491af2575ff6821b3f6cd71e15b2596d9f25b810f836f64ee327ae8628af', 'provisional_accept', '62352356a6dd1674e7b5fcff7dc6b161'),
('B', '357a5ca6-c0ff-4ad2-a5ed-1a2dfce4cf8d', 'A8', 'Looking at the force, it starts at 2.0 N and goes up to 14 N over 6 meters, so the force changes linearly with position. I can write this as F = 2.0 + 2.0x where x is in meters. Wait actually that doesn''t work because when x=6, that gives F = 2.0 + 12 = 14 N, yeah that''s right.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy1-frq-025", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-work", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-energy", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-speed", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "c-average", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "c-instant", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'f054ddd08b681af89e81d913c2f66a80fb8d544b8656ada217277d5f0fb7566a', 'reader_queue', '62352356a6dd1674e7b5fcff7dc6b161'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A1', 'Because the volume is constant, no work is done (W=0), so the heat added, Q, equals the change in internal energy, ΔU, by the first law of thermodynamics. For a monatomic ideal gas, ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT, so Q = (3/2)(1)(8.31)(60), which calculates to about 748 J, and this makes sense since an ideal gas''s internal energy is only a function of temperature, so all that heat directly increased its internal energy.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '3152d9a41414bf25c541a6f2fb7fd97738254b6089ceb730e6256656019d6608', 'provisional_accept', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A2', 'Since volume is fixed, the gas does no work, W = 0. So the first law gives ΔU = Q. For a monatomic ideal gas, internal energy is just (3/2)nRΔT, so Q = (3/2)(1)(8.314)(60) ≈ 748 J. The ideal gas assumption means there’s no intermolecular potential energy, and at constant volume no energy goes into pushing the piston, so all the heat goes straight into raising the kinetic energy of the molecules.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'c2f0898274cb9990198576736913fb7ff34a1f17b319680ef3579fbf0112dc97', 'provisional_accept', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A3', 'For part (a), at constant volume no work is done so W=0. Using the first law Q = ΔU + W, we get Q = ΔU. For a monatomic ideal gas, ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT = (3/2)(1 mol)(8.314 J/mol·K)(60 K) which gives ΔU ≈ 748 J, so Q ≈ 748 J.

For part (b), since the volume is constant the gas doesn''t expand or compress, meaning no work is done on or by the gas, so all the heat added just goes into increasing the internal energy of the gas molecules.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'f2a388495b3aea5049ebb749127dd49ed8be12e53f28654041b2c993828f93f2', 'reader_queue', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A4', 'The first law of thermodynamics says that the change in internal energy of the gas equals the heat added to it minus the work done by the gas. For a constant volume process, the gas can''t expand or contract, so no work is done by it, making the work term zero. This means all the added heat directly goes into changing the gas''s internal energy. Because it''s an ideal gas, its internal energy only depends on temperature, so adding heat at constant volume just increases its temperature and thus its internal energy.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '27d315040d161d2ec87f11a8a30ecfb960a2f9563b742feb960b83b02cdfdf46', 'provisional_accept', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A5', 'For constant volume, work is zero, so the first law gives ΔU = Q. For a monatomic ideal gas, ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT, so Q = (3/2)(1 mol)(8.314)(60 K) ≈ 748 J. But wait, I think you also have to consider the pressure change from the ideal gas law, so maybe you need to add PΔV, but since volume is constant, that term is zero, so yeah Q is just the internal energy change.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'e3297ae8dc158be3da44c281894c09a540627b0b0c3c3386ada13ca4e6466d86', 'reader_queue', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A6', '(a) For constant volume there''s no work done since W = PΔV and ΔV = 0, so W = 0. The internal energy change for a monatomic ideal gas is ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT = (3/2)(1 mol)(8.314 J/mol·K)(60 K) = 748 J. From the first law Q = ΔU + W, so Q = 748 + 0 = 748 J.

(b) Since the volume stays constant the gas doesn''t do any expansion work, and for ideal gases the internal energy is basically just related to temperature through the kinetic energy of the particles, so when we add heat it goes into increasing the internal energy since there''s no other place for it to go.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '27d5c99412c853041080f0a6d96f0bc377b163a4c5ebade9e3f20e6541b00836', 'reader_queue', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A7', 'Okay for part a, it''s constant volume so W=0, which means Q has to be equal to ΔU from the first law. Since it''s a monatomic ideal gas, ΔU = (3/2)nRΔT. We have n=1 mol, R=8.31 J/molK, and ΔT is 360-300=60K. So ΔU = (1.5)(1)(8.31)(60) which I got as around 740 J. So Q = 740 J.

For part b, if the volume is constant, the added heat just goes into the gas''s internal energy.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '3bfa2dba9f33d6b8c6811f9d20151baf8bf391505cbb916daba11fef4a43b7e1', 'provisional_accept', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'f2e357a2-b035-494e-a190-666b5af41639', 'A8', 'For an ideal monatomic gas at constant volume, W = 0 because volume doesn’t change. So from the first law, Q = ΔU. The internal energy for a monatomic ideal gas is U = (3/2)nRT, so ΔU = (3/2)(1)(8.314)(60) = 748 J. So Q = 748 J.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-001", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'c22ce1674f4b4bd9173d3b5c2a6ed4cfd531c8b9a921958aaf05bda603a4b46d', 'provisional_accept', '5b30384bdb007088f493ec94d32af34334863724c88bc5a770535fb64fbb1361'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A1', '**(a)** I would take the bar magnet and pull it through the coil at different constant speeds, maybe using a motion sensor or video to measure the speed each time. For each trial I''d record the maximum voltage reading from the sensor, keeping the magnet oriented the same way and going through the center of the coil each time.

**(b)** The independent variable is the magnet''s speed, and the dependent variable is the peak induced emf magnitude. I need to control the number of turns in the coil, the coil''s area, how close the magnet gets to the coil, and the magnet''s orientation and path. If I graph peak |emf| vs speed I expect it to be roughly linear since faster flux changes induce larger voltages.

**(c)** When the magnet enters the coil the voltage sensor will show a pulse in one direction (say positive), and when it exits there''ll be a pulse in the opposite direction (negative). This happens because the induced current opposes the flux change—when the magnet enters, flux is increasing so the induced current creates a field to oppose that increase, but when leaving the flux decreases so the current reverses to oppose the decrease instead.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5637fd8c716bd2774b2b55490d33b5f3d7d9793703ee42f13d7d4366598cf285', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A2', 'I''d take the same bar magnet and push it through the same coil along the same path, keeping its orientation fixed, at a few different speeds, making sure it gets equally close each time and recording the biggest voltage reading from the sensor for each speed. Then I''d graph the absolute value of that peak voltage against the measured speed, expecting to see a pretty linear relationship since speed is what I''m changing and the peak voltage is what I''m measuring. When the magnet enters, the voltage sensor will show a pulse, say positive, because the increasing magnetic flux through the coil induces a current to create a field opposing this increase, but when the magnet leaves, the flux decreases, so the induced current flips direction to create a field that tries to maintain the original flux, leading to a negative voltage pulse, opposite to the first one.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '6ee686eeff9ab06dac962b9a93ca6046d9b865ea14faf75e9d92456da4485cf4', 'provisional_accept', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A3', 'We set up the coil and voltage sensor, then pushed the same magnet through at different speeds—slow, medium, fast—and recorded the peak voltage each time. We kept the magnet orientation and the path the same, and made sure it entered from the same distance each trial so the closest approach was fixed. Then we plotted peak voltage magnitude on the y-axis against speed on the x-axis, expecting a roughly straight line through the origin. The voltage pulses are opposite because when the magnet enters, the flux increases and the induced current makes a field opposing that increase, but when it leaves, flux decreases and the current opposes that decrease, so the signs flip.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '91875a2c2b99adf9d42422654995cc9566366e2246a7adbe75d67c3c0b0fabb0', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A4', 'For the procedure I would drop the magnet through the coil from different heights so it has different speeds when it passes through, keeping the magnet orientation the same each time. The voltage sensor would record the voltage and I''d measure how high the peaks are for each drop height.

When the magnet enters the coil the flux is increasing so by Lenz''s law the induced current opposes this change, creating a voltage in one direction. When it leaves the flux is decreasing so the induced current opposes that change by trying to maintain the flux, which means the current flows the opposite way and you get opposite sign voltage pulses.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '23a42af5eaca8d601440b18a3837ca8ba3a478d5d2ae52e3f93d05e0a0fb58a2', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A5', 'We''d push the magnet through the coil at different speeds, making sure to use the same magnet and coil each time. We should also try to vary the distance the magnet gets to the coil walls to see if that makes a difference, and measure the peak voltage. The magnet speed is the independent variable, and the peak magnitude of the emf is dependent. If we plot peak |emf| vs. speed, it should be pretty linear. When the magnet enters, say a North pole, the flux increases, so by Lenz''s law, the induced current creates a field to oppose this, leading to a positive voltage pulse. When it leaves, the flux decreases, so the induced current makes a field to try and maintain the flux, causing a negative pulse, so they''re opposite.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'da48c3bd1eb2a43386c4deb98168ec079222ef48215bc2ff119f7eb9add880bb', 'provisional_accept', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A6', 'ok so for part (a) i’d take the same magnet and same coil, hold the magnet orientation the same, and push it through the coil at different speeds like slow, medium, fast, measuring the peak voltage each time. i’d keep the coil fixed and make sure the magnet enters from the same side and goes the same distance past the coil, also keeping the closest approach the same. then i’d plot peak voltage magnitude versus speed, and it should come out roughly linear over the speeds i test if i don’t go too crazy fast. the independent variable is speed, dependent is the peak emf magnitude.

for the trace, when the magnet enters the coil the flux increases in one direction so the induced current makes a field opposing that, giving one sign pulse, and when it leaves the flux decreases so the induced current flips to oppose that decrease, which should give the opposite sign. so the two pulses are basically mirror images of each other on the voltage vs time graph.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'c141286ea2efe27dde6fa6791223eb54f89889d8ec7d6a6c1bc46b446ace11ad', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A7', 'For the procedure, I''d move the magnet through the coil at different speeds - like maybe 0.5 m/s, 1 m/s, 1.5 m/s, etc - and record the peak voltage each time. The independent variable is speed and dependent is peak emf magnitude, and I''d graph peak |emf| vs speed which should be roughly linear since faster motion means faster flux change. When the magnet enters the coil, flux is increasing so by Lenz''s law the induced current opposes that increase, creating voltage in one direction, but when it exits the flux decreases so the induced current opposes the decrease which makes the voltage flip to the opposite sign - that''s why you get opposite polarity pulses for entering vs leaving.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'c6edb0b6baa53a745d0e73ab80df9972f6851bc0d60c37336ebb13e055ea515b', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', 'a27d83da-70a5-497d-95f5-faf833e820a8', 'A8', 'I''d use the same magnet and coil, moving the magnet through at different speeds. I''d record the magnet''s speed and the peak voltage each time. The variables are the magnet''s speed and the peak voltage, and I would graph the results.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphy2-frq-027", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-procedure", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-controls", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-graph", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-variables", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "c-lenz", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "c-pulses", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'b66dadf6b723cce63e3a9e6cc161cee362de60e59fbb0ec4b4d1e772f682325b', 'reader_queue', 'c982d46c7f62815d453d13226d27e90e'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A2', '(a) So when you add charge dq to the capacitor the voltage is q/C at that moment, meaning the work needed is V·dq = (q/C)dq. Integrating from zero charge up to Q gives U = ∫₀^Q (q/C)dq = q²/(2C)|₀^Q = Q²/(2C). Since the capacitance is C = εA/d we get U = Q²d/(2εA).

(b) The work-energy relation dU=(q/C)dq is basically saying each bit of charge requires work equal to the instantaneous voltage times dq, and that work becomes stored energy. The key thing is we''re assuming C=εA/d stays constant the whole time we''re charging it up—if capacitance changed during charging we couldn''t pull C out of the integral, but since it''s constant we can treat it like a constant and integrate q/C directly to get q²/(2C), which gives us the Q²/(2C) result.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'd9b3509a19958b149d15edcdf22caaf60c3993dac1123446960148236a2a8c85', 'provisional_accept', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A3', 'To find the energy stored, we use the work done to charge it, dU = Vdq. Since V=q/C, that makes dU = (q/C)dq. Integrating from 0 to Q, with C constant, we get U = ∫₀^Q (q/C)dq = (1/C) [q²/2]₀^Q = Q²/(2C). And the capacitance C is εA/d, so the energy is Q²d/(2εA).', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '56dc12e4a01a875119a1e547cfb9dc13c0c86d0c627c572a8a06473f80f8fbb4', 'provisional_accept', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A4', 'We start with the work done to move a tiny bit of charge dq onto the plates, which is dU = V dq, and since V = q/C, that gives dU = (q/C)dq. Then you just add up all those little bits from q=0 to q=Q, and because C stays the same the whole time (the plate area, distance, and dielectric don’t change), you can pull 1/C out of the integral. So U = (1/C)∫₀^Q q dq = (1/C)(Q²/2) = Q²/(2C). That’s the energy, and the whole thing relies on C not changing as you charge it.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '492f4fd9775340f783e121276c6df6cc19037bbe7a20f7382923a8b96c8b51f2', 'reader_queue', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A5', 'Looking at part (a), the energy stored comes from integrating the work done to add charge. When you add a small amount of charge dq to the capacitor that already has charge q on it, the voltage is V = q/C, so the work is dU = V dq = (q/C)dq. Integrating from 0 to Q gives U = ∫₀^Q (q/C)dq = q²/(2C)|₀^Q = Q²/(2C). Using C = εA/d, this becomes U = Q²d/(2εA).

For part (b), the work-energy relation dU = (q/C)dq works because as we charge the capacitor the voltage increases linearly with q, but actually wait – if C stayed constant that means the voltage would have to change, so the dielectric constant ε must be adjusting during charging to keep C the same even though q is increasing. This keeps the energy expression valid throughout the charging process.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'b9163ed1b225b523e7cd9c52e5e83e1523671d03856a28317764765f5a513357', 'provisional_accept', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A6', 'The energy stored in the capacitor is found by integrating the work done to add charge, so dU = Vdq. Since V = q/C, then U = ∫₀^Q (q/C)dq. Integrating this with C constant gives U = Q²/(2C). For a parallel-plate capacitor, C is given by εA/d, so you can also write the energy as U = Q²d/(2εA). The dU=(q/C)dq expression is pretty fundamental for the work, and C=εA/d sets up what the capacitance is, so they kinda just fit together to get that energy.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '838458e5d4a431126ead45747203f214c8b759d43cab2b57ee77a61892713372', 'provisional_accept', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '82301201-c50d-4db5-b09e-79986508c320', 'A8', '**(a)** So to find the energy stored, I need to integrate the work done charging it up. The work to add a little charge dq when there''s already charge q on it is dW = V dq = (q/C)dq. So integrating from 0 to Q gives U = ∫₀^Q (q/C)dq = q²/(2C)|₀^Q = Q²/(2C). Since C = εA/d, that''s the energy stored.

**(b)** The work-energy relation dU = (q/C)dq shows how each bit of charge requires work against the existing voltage... wait actually I''m not sure this makes sense because if the capacitance changes while charging then the whole integral would be different and we''d get a totally different answer.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-003", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '9ad22ab343e33fd165d106817cf64158597143b3d402e36d24d4427a0bed06f1', 'provisional_accept', 'b488ece15e547d371910f5b33fee60ad'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A1', 'To test Gauss''s law, you''d select a closed surface and approximate the flux by summing E·n ΔA over many small surface elements. For each element, you take the E-field from the model and the outward normal vector, then calculate E·n ΔA and add them all up to get the total net flux. You then compare this calculated net flux with the enclosed charge divided by epsilon naught. For controls, the enclosed charge should remain fixed for a given surface. You''d refine the surface elements, making them smaller, until the flux estimate stabilizes, which shows convergence. For any closed surface enclosing zero net charge, the expected result is that the net flux will be approximately zero, even if there are strong local E-fields.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '41849ec564313f6b97d1734fd4ad52a92b40f2af08838d7c761614746aa61548', 'provisional_accept', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A2', 'So basically I’d pick a closed surface, split it into a bunch of tiny patches, and for each patch multiply the local E field’s component normal to the patch by the patch area, then add all those up to get the net flux. Then I’d compare that number to Q_inside / ε₀ — they should match within rounding error. For a surface with no charge inside, even if E is strong near it, the flux should come out to nearly zero, and I’d keep shrinking the patch size until the flux number stops changing (while the enclosed charge stays the same), which is my convergence check.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '36b0fc09d313ed15418020e0149416fe29bf17ff57ab4295b18855f73bad9637', 'provisional_accept', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A3', 'For part (a), I would draw several different closed surfaces of varying sizes and shapes around the charged object. Then I''d break each surface into small area elements ΔA, multiply the normal component of E at each element by ΔA to get E·n ΔA, and sum all these up to get the total flux through that surface. Compare this calculated flux to the enclosed charge divided by ε₀ to see if Gauss''s law holds.

For part (b), I should probably keep the grid resolution the same for consistency as a control. For surfaces that don''t enclose any charge, even though the electric field might be non-zero on the surface, the net flux should be approximately zero since Φ = Q_enc/ε₀ and Q_enc = 0.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '1322f0d38372f3f67b0e0e071937be67dfeab00439ddcccdf5d0d49ca3c46678', 'provisional_accept', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A4', 'You would pick different closed surfaces, like a box or a sphere around the charge, then break each surface into many tiny area elements, ΔA. For each little element, you get the electric field vector E and its outward normal vector n, then compute E dot n times ΔA, summing all these values over the whole surface to get the total flux. To make sure this flux value is accurate, you''d keep refining the surface elements, making them smaller and smaller, until the calculated total flux doesn''t change much anymore. For these tests, you''d keep the charged object fixed, so the enclosed charge remains the same for a given surface.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A4", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'ad2a3875f6af6a9203b7ef3e2623a1b92411d3de85088331dcf9c9868b501fcd', 'provisional_accept', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A5', 'So for each surface I’d take the electric field values on the grid points, and basically do a rough sum of E times the area of each little patch, but I’d refine the grid until the number stops changing much. Then I’d compare that to the total charge inside divided by epsilon0, and they should match if Gauss’s law holds. For surfaces with no charge inside, even if there’s field passing through, the net flux should come out to about zero—I’d check that by making sure the enclosed charge stays zero while I shrink or change the surface shape, and the flux should converge to something near zero. I think if I just keep refining the patch sizes, the sum will get closer to zero for the no-charge case, but honestly the field isn’t exactly zero so it might not be exactly zero.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '75933ee89ddbe23af9607c08001ab80626d41077ce5ffb681dd7fb770263590b', 'reader_queue', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A6', '**(a)** For each surface, I''d divide it into small patches with area ΔA and find the normal vector n at each patch. Then calculate E·n at each patch from the model data and sum up E·n ΔA over all patches to get the total flux. Compare this flux to Q_enc/ε₀ where Q_enc is the charge inside that surface.

**(b)** As a control, use surfaces at different distances and shapes around the same charge to verify consistency. For convergence, I''d make the grid spacing smaller and check that results become more consistent. For surfaces with no charge inside, the net flux should be approximately zero even though E isn''t zero at the surface since field lines pass through without originating inside.', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'a7b0194153a8a77a0c4f2f845d1c72772395a07d323cf46128d58e15ccf73cd0', 'reader_queue', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A7', 'To test Gauss''s law, we first use the model to determine the net electric flux through a chosen surface and find the total charge enclosed. This calculated flux is then compared to the enclosed charge divided by *two times* epsilon naught. For controls, we''d use various surface sizes and shapes, always knowing the net charge inside. A convergence check involves refining surface resolution until the computed net flux stabilizes, with the enclosed charge held fixed. For surfaces enclosing no net charge, we''d expect the net flux to be approximately zero, even if E-fields are present.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '2f3b020bc8a64bf95a118275b26f768b4bb22b849863f2266232fe06b885a388', 'reader_queue', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', '40fe3572-9729-4159-8f0d-4f34b8862233', 'A8', 'So for Gauss’s law, I’d just calculate the net electric flux through each closed surface by adding up the dot product of E and the area vector for each little patch, then compare that to the enclosed charge divided by ε₀. For surfaces with no enclosed charge, I’d keep the surface the same but make the grid finer to see if the flux value changes, and it should stay basically zero since there’s no charge inside. But wait, actually if the local field is nonzero, the flux might not be exactly zero—oh well, it’s close enough.', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycem-frq-019", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-gauss-comparison", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-sum", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-empty", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-quality", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '947dabe7158501143ccdd5bdaf4eed00f4a7dae58d80cf9d3668ec89151813a4', 'reader_queue', 'dd80c4a0be7cddd7088a249dddbbd542'),
('B', 'afa0244f-56c0-4514-b4f9-5fff9013631a', 'A1', '**Part (a)**

Starting with Newton''s second law: m dv/dt = -bv. I can separate variables to get dv/v = -(b/m)dt, then integrate both sides. The left side gives ln(v) - ln(v₀) and the right side gives -bt/m, so ln(v/v₀) = -bt/m, which means v(t) = v₀e^(-bt/m). For position, I integrate velocity: x(t) = ∫v₀e^(-bt/m)dt = v₀(-m/b)e^(-bt/m) + C. Using x(0)=0 gives C = mv₀/b, so x(t) = (mv₀/b)(1 - e^(-bt/m)).

**Part (b)**

The equation m dv/dt = -bv shows that the rate of change of velocity is proportional to the velocity itself (with negative constant -b/m), which is the defining characteristic of exponential decay. When you have something that decreases at a rate proportional to its current value, you always get an exponential function as the solution, so v(t) has to be of the form v₀e^(-bt/m).', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-002", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '5dcef30b98d298c68aecfb47b004f86b538f5ae9f743f638c09fdf5ead3f4168', 'provisional_accept', '050bd855baa1c26737dd780de1613e9d'),
('B', 'afa0244f-56c0-4514-b4f9-5fff9013631a', 'A2', 'Separating variables in m dv/dt = -bv to get dv/v = (-b/m)dt, then integrating from v₀ to v and 0 to t, we get v(t)=v₀e^{-bt/m}. Then just integrate v(t) again, using x(0)=0, to find x(t)=mv₀(1-e^{-bt/m})/b. The exponential shape for v(t) comes directly from how its rate of change (dv/dt) is proportional to v itself; this means it decays faster when it''s faster, but then slows its decay as v drops, which is exactly how exponential functions work when the exponent is negative.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-002", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, 'a78d6aacebe2225abdee2b2c1f09b7e28c33a9e518912fb96821d818033c31d0', 'provisional_accept', '050bd855baa1c26737dd780de1613e9d'),
('B', 'afa0244f-56c0-4514-b4f9-5fff9013631a', 'A5', 'Okay so for (a), m dv/dt = -bv, which means dv/v = -b/m dt, and integrating that gives ln(v) - ln(v₀) = -bt/m, so v(t) = v₀e^(-bt/m). Then integrating v(t) with x(0)=0 gives x(t) = (mv₀/b)(1 - e^(-bt/m)). For (b), the exponential solution happens because the drag force F=-bv means that the acceleration is also proportional to -v, so like, it just naturally decays exponentially because the force itself gets smaller as the velocity gets smaller.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-002", "answer_type": "A5", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '5df984128f4a69f72cefde7ee83e6400505d33345341916a513542da500f7a3a', 'provisional_accept', '050bd855baa1c26737dd780de1613e9d'),
('B', 'afa0244f-56c0-4514-b4f9-5fff9013631a', 'A8', 'So you start with F=ma, which is -bv = m dv/dt. To get v(t), you separate the variables, integrate, and you find v(t) = v₀e^(-bt/m). Then integrate that v(t) from t=0 to some time t to get x(t), setting x(0)=0, which works out to be x(t) = mv₀(1-e^(-bt/m))/b.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-002", "answer_type": "A8", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "part-a", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "part-b", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '06fa121201820ecfd560890c022892abd05e024301ec1fb7ab2c0314e9765c8f', 'provisional_accept', '050bd855baa1c26737dd780de1613e9d'),
('B', 'aa3f2f91-39ff-4d42-9af4-9648fdf495ce', 'A1', 'Okay so first, the forces are just tension along the string and mg straight down. Vertical: F_T cosθ = mg, and radial inward: F_T sinθ = mω²(L sinθ). Then dividing the radial by the vertical gives tanθ = ω²L/g, so ω = sqrt(g/(L cosθ)). Then P = 2π/ω = 2π sqrt(L cosθ/g).', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-019", "answer_type": "A1", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-equations", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-forces", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-angular-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-period", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '15be506d69d0f5ec60cf8e8dbf50e1dc0436d346ae53916ba370fc0686f0e654', 'provisional_accept', 'df1cacecbd6ff32b0c010b89a21fc716'),
('B', 'aa3f2f91-39ff-4d42-9af4-9648fdf495ce', 'A2', '**Part (a)**

So the forces are mg pointing down and the string tension pointing up along the string at angle theta. For the vertical I''ll call that the y-direction and horizontal inward is the r-direction. Since it''s going in a circle at constant height, the y-component has zero acceleration so F_T cos(theta) = mg. In the radial direction pointing toward the center, there''s centripetal acceleration so F_T sin(theta) = m omega^2 r where r = L sin(theta).

**Part (b)**

OK so from part (a) I can divide the second equation by the first to get rid of tension: tan(theta) = omega^2(L sin(theta))/g. Then sin(theta)/cos(theta) = omega^2(L sin(theta))/g, the sin(theta) cancels and I get 1/cos(theta) = omega^2 L/g, so omega^2 = g/(L cos(theta)) which means omega = sqrt(g/(L cos(theta))). The period is the time for one full rotation so P = 2pi/omega = 2pi/sqrt(g/(L cos(theta))) = 2pi sqrt(L cos(theta)/g).', 'anthropic', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-019", "answer_type": "A2", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-equations", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-forces", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-angular-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-period", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '9055e98b68f890b20269bc082520900b0e357ab1ad2a8b821da9e89d560c4e27', 'provisional_accept', 'df1cacecbd6ff32b0c010b89a21fc716'),
('B', 'aa3f2f91-39ff-4d42-9af4-9648fdf495ce', 'A3', 'Okay, so for the forces, you have the string tension $F_T$ going along the string, and then gravity $mg$ straight down. I''ll take up as positive y and inward as positive radial. Vertically, $F_T \cos(\theta)$ points up, so $F_T \cos(\theta) - mg = 0$, no acceleration there. Radially, $F_T \sin(\theta)$ points inward, so $F_T \sin(\theta) = m \omega^2 (L \sin(\theta))$ since the radius is $L \sin(\theta)$. From the vertical part, $F_T = mg/\cos(\theta)$. Plugging that into the radial equation gives $(mg/\cos(\theta)) \sin(\theta) = m \omega^2 L \sin(\theta)$. The $m$ and $\sin(\theta)$ cancel out, leaving $g/\cos(\theta) = \omega^2 L$, so $\omega = \sqrt{g/(L \cos(\theta))}$.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-019", "answer_type": "A3", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-equations", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-forces", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-angular-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-period", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, 'd2693c0bfd143579b2a15a61bb18b2ca74d3d6957680cd294e1b29ea4bc61adf', 'provisional_accept', 'df1cacecbd6ff32b0c010b89a21fc716'),
('B', 'aa3f2f91-39ff-4d42-9af4-9648fdf495ce', 'A6', 'The FBD shows tension `F_T` acting along the string and weight `mg` straight down. For vertical equilibrium (y-direction), `F_T cos(theta) = mg`. In the inward radial direction, the net force causes centripetal acceleration, so `F_T sin(theta) = m omega^2 (L sin(theta))`. Dividing these equations lets you get `omega = sqrt(g / (L cos(theta)))`. The orbital period P would be related to this angular speed.', 'google', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-019", "answer_type": "A6", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-equations", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-forces", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-angular-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-period", "present": false}]}'::jsonb, '22e741daeb445d90c27241cd1209cf18b7e1407bade1c69ee860c49c3aa2411d', 'provisional_accept', 'df1cacecbd6ff32b0c010b89a21fc716'),
('B', 'aa3f2f91-39ff-4d42-9af4-9648fdf495ce', 'A7', 'The tension has to have both a vertical and a radial part. Vertically, \(F_T \cos\theta = mg\) since there’s no up/down motion, and radially inward, \(F_T \sin\theta = m\omega^2 (L\sin\theta)\). Dividing the radial by the vertical gives \(\tan\theta = \frac{\omega^2 L \sin\theta}{g}\), so \(\omega = \sqrt{\frac{g}{L\cos\theta}}\). Then \(P = 2\pi/\omega = 2\pi \sqrt{\frac{L\cos\theta}{g}}\).', 'deepseek', '{"content_key": "apphycm-frq-019", "answer_type": "A7", "expected": [{"criterion_key": "a-equations", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "a-forces", "present": false}, {"criterion_key": "b-angular-speed", "present": true}, {"criterion_key": "b-period", "present": true}]}'::jsonb, '1cc0924c157d10529c2bf9afd27f3ce4f3b0ca54b37e13cec1ac87a8c9912295', 'provisional_accept', 'df1cacecbd6ff32b0c010b89a21fc716');